A||||9||SCCA FIERO||Bushing came out easy...||||14||
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||03-26-2000||04:29 PM||||I have an extra set of trailing arms and lateral links for my 88. I thought I would strip them and paint them to be nice and pertty. I started to burn out the bushings when I got an idea. :::light bulb above head::: <P>I stuck a drill bit between the steel sleeve and the bushing and turned it on. It spun around the sleeve. I grabbed the sleeve with pliers and pulled it out. Then I did the same around the outside of the bushing then hit it with a hammer... TA DA! Took me about 3 minutes to do both bushings in that trailing arm. I hope the front will be this easy. ||||reg||14
Z||000001||TheFiero64||03-26-2000||06:59 PM||||Thanks for the suggestion. I am gona be putting in the poly bushings soon & will try your method. Sounds better than burning them <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||03-26-2000||09:06 PM||||I used some various sized sockets and my trusty vise to push the old bushings out. If someone had a press, it would be really easy. But then again, my bushings were actually falling apart.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-26-2000||11:23 PM||||I took mine to work and soaked the ends down with Liquid Nitrogen. Once the rubber bushings were frozen, I just "tapped" them with a hammer and they cracked and fell on the floor. After they defrosted I pressed in the poly bushings. Total time for upper and lower fronts was 30 minutes!||||reg||3
Z||000004||Ozzy||03-27-2000||12:24 AM||||My wife is sitting next to me and she just read this post. (She helped me replace the uppers and lowers on the '88 Coupe)<P>She says <B>"Ya'wl SUCK"</B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (Joking!!)<P>We spent hours burning those suckers out and ended up with black goo from A**hole to elbow!<P>I luv the Nitrogen idea...can you buy it for non commercial purposes anywhere...you know, so folks can freeze fingers off and stuff!!<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||03-27-2000||12:54 AM||||I burned my front a-arm bushings out and swore that was the last time I would do that.  I used a drill on the rear a-arms and front cradle bushings and it took about 1/10th the time and was much much cleaner.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-27-2000||01:03 PM||||call around to welding and cryo supliers. They are all in the phone book. Some will sell to the public some won't. You might could do this with dry ice. You can get that at allot of bulk ice deallers, and some Jack & Jill warehouses. Even if the dry ice won't make the rubber crack it will be easier to cut/drill.<P>The only thing you have to watch out for is that the outer sleeve could shink enough to fall out of the A-arm or whatever. If that happens chill the sleeve again and hold it where it belongs untill it warms up. Let it warm on it's own. (Don't rechill the arm. just the sleeve)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||coyote||03-27-2000||10:46 PM||||this is how i get them out, i use a propane torch and heat the outer sleeve, if you watch carefully you will se the bushing start to pop out of the sleeve. Heat the sleeve evenly. the bushing will start to outgas a little. typically at this point you can push them out with a srcewdriver. it takes me about a half hour to do a complete set, front and back.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||03-27-2000||10:59 PM||||I used coyote's method, worked ok for me.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||03-27-2000||11:11 PM||||An advantage of the cryo methods is that it doesn't screw up the tempering of the metals.||||reg||1
